From: Hideo Sumi <hideo.sumi@mugops.com>
Date: Sat, 30 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: ipq5332: add QPIC BAM and SPI NAND
 controller nodes

IPQ5332 embeds the same QPIC block as IPQ9574/IPQ5018, already
described upstream for those SoCs as qpic_nand: spi@79b0000 (driven by
spi-qpic-snand.c, both already present in this kernel version), but
upstream's ipq5332.dtsi does not describe this controller yet. Add it
here, mirroring ipq9574.dtsi's node exactly (register layout, clocks,
QPIC BAM), with the ipq5332-specific compatible and qpic_bam interrupt
number.

This corrects an earlier, wrong attempt at the same board bring-up
that assumed the board's QPIC block drives a raw parallel NAND chip
("qcom,ipq5332-nand", handled by qcom_nandc.c) rather than a serial
(SPI) NAND chip. That assumption was made from the vendor devicetree's
"qcom,ipq5332-nand" compatible string alone; it did not hold up against
the real hardware. A live vendor dmesg on the actual device reads:

    nand: device found, Manufacturer ID: 0xef, Chip ID: 0xba
    nand: Winbond W25N01GWZEIG SPI NAND 1G 1.8V
    nand: 128 MiB, SLC, erase size: 128 KiB, page size: 2048, OOB size: 64

and the vendor devicetree's own pinmux node for this controller is
named "qspi_nand_pins" (qspi_clk/qspi_cs) -- genuine QSPI signals, not
an 8-bit parallel NAND bus. The Winbond W25N01GW (ID 0xba/0x21) is a
standard part already supported by the generic
drivers/mtd/nand/spi/winbond.c. qcom,ipq5332-nand (without "-snand")
was in fact never added to upstream's raw-NAND qcom,nandc.yaml
compatible list for IPQ5332 -- the vendor's QSDK downstream kernel
evidently reuses that compatible name for its own unified NAND driver
that autodetects chip type at runtime, unrelated to upstream's
raw/serial split.

qpic_bam's interrupt number (66) is IPQ5332-specific and comes from
the vendor devicetree's dma@7984000 node
("interrupts = <0x00 0x42 0x04>"); IPQ9574 uses a different number
(146) for the same BAM at the same register offset, so it cannot be
copied across SoCs.

Signed-off-by: Hideo Sumi <hideo.sumi@mugops.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/ipq5332.dtsi | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/ipq5332.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/ipq5332.dtsi
@@ -687,6 +687,33 @@
 			};
 		};

+		qpic_bam: dma-controller@7984000 {
+			compatible = "qcom,bam-v1.7.4", "qcom,bam-v1.7.0";
+			reg = <0x07984000 0x1c000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 66 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&gcc GCC_QPIC_AHB_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "bam_clk";
+			#dma-cells = <1>;
+			qcom,ee = <0>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
+		qpic_nand: spi@79b0000 {
+			compatible = "qcom,ipq5332-snand", "qcom,ipq9574-snand";
+			reg = <0x079b0000 0x10000>;
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <0>;
+			clocks = <&gcc GCC_QPIC_CLK>,
+				 <&gcc GCC_QPIC_AHB_CLK>,
+				 <&gcc GCC_QPIC_IO_MACRO_CLK>;
+			clock-names = "core", "aon", "iom";
+			dmas = <&qpic_bam 0>,
+			       <&qpic_bam 1>,
+			       <&qpic_bam 2>;
+			dma-names = "tx", "rx", "cmd";
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
 		usb: usb@8af8800 {
 			compatible = "qcom,ipq5332-dwc3", "qcom,dwc3";
 			reg = <0x08af8800 0x400>;
